# ExSuperior Mobile Application — Technical Design

Status: Proposed  
Target platforms: iOS and Android  
Product language/direction: Hebrew, right-to-left  
Web application source: `/Users/adirsolomon/Development/PHP/exsuperior`

## 1. Purpose

Build a production-grade mobile application that provides feature parity with the existing non-dashboard ExSuperior experience on iPhone and Android. The existing Laravel application remains the system of record. A new versioned JSON API exposes the same domain behavior to a native cross-platform client.

This design is based on the current routes, controllers, requests, policies, models, React pages, and feature tests in the repository. The README's statement that the product uses static demo content is outdated and is not used as the scope definition.

## 2. Goals

- Deliver one shared TypeScript codebase producing native iOS and Android binaries.
- Preserve the current Hebrew RTL experience, permissions, privacy modes, moderation rules, and authorization policies.
- Cover all user-facing capabilities outside `/dashboard`.
- Keep the web and mobile clients behaviorally consistent through shared Laravel domain services and API Resources.
- Support secure device authentication, media capture/upload, push notifications, universal/app links, resilient reads, local drafts, accessibility, observability, and store delivery.
- Add contract, backend, application, and end-to-end tests so parity is measurable.

## 3. Non-goals

- Rebuilding the administrator dashboard in the mobile application.
- Replacing the existing Laravel/Inertia web application.
- Sharing web React UI components with React Native. Domain terminology and contracts are shared; UI implementations are native.
- Reproducing web-only SEO, sitemap, robots, service-worker installation, or browser cookie-banner behavior.
- Making server-authoritative writes fully offline. The app may preserve a draft and retry explicitly, but it must not silently replay non-idempotent social actions.

